course: history
lesson: 86
title: Olympiad: cause and effect chains ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A cause is what makes something happen; an effect is what follows from it. History is full of chains like a line of dominoes: push the first one, and the last one falls far away, years or even centuries later.
explain: A chain from the start of the course: farming gave people food they could store, villages grew into cities, rulers had to count grain, animals and taxes, and so writing began in Sumer.
explain: Trap one: «after» does not always mean «because». Shevchenko's «Kobzar» came out in 1840, a year after the First Opium War began, but neither had anything to do with the other. Look for how one thing really pushed the next.
explain: Trap two: big events rarely have only one cause. The shot in Sarajevo in 1914 was the spark of the First World War, but two rival alliances of big powers were the dry wood waiting for it.
explain: Hamish in Aberdeen tells his wee sister: «Dinnae just say what happened, say why, ken» (dinnae means don't, wee means little). Olympiad puzzles hand you a chain in a muddle: find the first push, then follow each «so...» to the end.
fact: Farming let people store food, villages grew into cities, and writing began in Sumer about 5,000 years ago, mostly to keep records of grain, animals and taxes.
fact: Roman roads were built so legions could march fast, and the same roads carried traders, the Latin language and later Christianity.
fact: The Mongol Empire made the Silk Road safer for traders such as Marco Polo, but trade routes from Asia also helped carry the Black Death towards Europe in the 1340s.
fact: The Black Death killed perhaps a third or more of Europe's people; with fewer workers left, survivors could ask for higher wages, and the old serf system weakened in western Europe.
fact: Gutenberg's printing press, from around 1450, made books much cheaper; more people learned to read, and new ideas, like Martin Luther's in 1517, spread across Europe within weeks.
fact: After 1492 diseases such as smallpox, carried by Europeans, killed very many native people of the Americas, who had never met them before, and this helped the Spanish conquer the Aztecs and the Inca.
fact: The first steam engines pumped water out of coal mines; more coal powered factories and railways, people moved to the towns, and crowded, dirty towns suffered from cholera.
fact: In 1854 the doctor John Snow mapped cholera cases in a London street and showed they gathered round one water pump; after the «Great Stink» of the Thames in 1858, London built big new sewers.
fact: Railways needed timetables, so from the 1840s British railways used London time everywhere, and towns gave up their own local clock times.
fact: Britain bought lots of tea from China and paid in silver; British traders then sold the harmful drug opium in China to get silver back, China banned it, and the Opium War of 1839–1842 ended with Hong Kong going to Britain.
fact: During the First World War women did many jobs of the men at the front; this, together with decades of campaigning by suffragists and suffragettes, helped some British women win the vote in 1918.
fact: The Soviet satellite Sputnik in 1957 shocked the United States, which set up NASA in 1958 and raced the Soviet Union to the Moon, landing there in 1969.
fact: In 1838 artists raffled Briullov's portrait of the poet Zhukovsky to buy Shevchenko out of serfdom; once free, he studied at the Academy of Arts and published his «Kobzar» in 1840.
exercise: Put the chain in order: writing begins; farming; cities grow; grain must be counted. | Farming, cities grow, grain must be counted, writing begins | Writing begins, farming, cities grow, grain must be counted | Cities grow, farming, writing begins, grain must be counted | Farming, writing begins, grain must be counted, cities grow
exercise: Why did writing begin in Sumer, mostly? | To keep records of grain, animals and taxes | To write down long poems and fairy tales | To send letters to friends in other lands | Because the gods gave people an alphabet
exercise: What did Roman roads carry besides the legions? | Traders, Latin and later Christianity | Only soldiers; all others were banned | Steam trains and carts with engines | Nothing; roads were for parades only
exercise: What helped bring the Black Death towards Europe? | Trade routes from Asia | Gutenberg's printing press | The new steam railways | The ships of Columbus
exercise: After the Black Death, why could workers ask for higher wages? | So many had died that workers were scarce | Kings passed laws doubling everyone's pay | Gold was found in nearly every village | The plague had made people much stronger
exercise: What followed straight on from printing making books cheaper? | More people learned to read | Fewer books were ever written | Paper was invented in China | The western Roman Empire fell
exercise: Why did Luther's ideas spread so fast in 1517? | They were printed in thousands of copies | Monks copied them all by hand in one night | They were sent round by telephone | Radio broadcast them across Europe
exercise: Why did so many native people of the Americas die after 1492? | They had never met diseases like smallpox | They all sailed away to Europe with Columbus | Volcanoes erupted in every land at once | They stopped farming to go exploring
exercise: What job did the first steam engines do? | Pumped water out of coal mines | Pulled the first passenger trains | Powered the first aeroplanes | Lit the streets of London at night
exercise: Which chain is in the right order? | Coal and steam, factories, crowded towns, cholera, sewers | Sewers, cholera, factories, coal and steam, crowded towns | Crowded towns, sewers, coal and steam, cholera, factories | Cholera, coal and steam, sewers, factories, crowded towns
exercise: What did John Snow show in 1854? | Cholera came from one water pump | Cholera was spread by bad smells | Cholera was carried by train rats | Cholera was caused by cold weather
exercise: Why did British towns give up their own clock times? | Trains needed one time | The king hated sundials | Clocks got far too dear | Factories stopped at noon
exercise: Why did British traders sell opium in China? | To get back silver paid for tea | Chinese doctors begged for it | The emperor ordered it as a gift | To swap it for Hong Kong at once
exercise: What did the Opium War of 1839–1842 lead to? | Hong Kong went to Britain | China came to rule Britain | Tea was banned in Britain | Opium was banned in Britain
exercise: What did Sputnik in 1957 lead to? | NASA and the race to the Moon | The end of the Second World War | The first powered aeroplane flight | The World Wide Web, that same year
exercise: How was Shevchenko freed from serfdom in 1838? | Artists raffled a portrait to buy his freedom | The tsar freed him as a prize for his poems | He ran away south to the Zaporozhian Sich | He won a big poetry prize in faraway Paris
exercise: Which pair shows «after» but NOT «because»? | The «Kobzar» in 1840, after the Opium War began | London's sewers, after cholera in its streets | NASA in 1958, after Sputnik went up in 1957 | Higher wages, after the Black Death killed many
test: ★★★ Mason in Boston: «Wicked simple: one dude got shot in Sarajevo, so the whole world went to war.» Best reply? | The shot was a spark; rival alliances pulled countries in | Yeah, one shot and nothing else, that's the whole story | No, the war began when Britain invaded Sarajevo | No, that shot came four years after the war ended
test: ★★★ Aoife in Galway: «Sure, people learned to read first, then books got cheap, then printing came along.» Fix the order. | Printing, cheap books, more readers | Readers, cheap books, then printing | Cheap books, printing, then readers | Printing, readers, then the books got dear
test: ★★★ Kiara in Melbourne: «Plague killed heaps of people, so workers got paid heaps less after, yeah nah?» What really happened? | Fewer workers, so survivors could ask more | Right, wages fell, as nobody needed workers | Wages stayed exactly the same for 500 years | Workers were paid in bread, not in money
test: ★★★ Ethan reckons: «The Spanish won in America just coz they were way stronger, period.» What's missing? | Smallpox had already killed huge numbers of native people | Nothing; a few hundred soldiers alone could easily beat millions | The Aztecs had no cities, no farms and no armies | The Inca invited the Spanish in to rule them
test: ★★★ A London dad in 1858: «Bit of a whiff off the river today.» The Thames stank so badly it was called the Great Stink. What came of it? | London built big new sewers | London moved to a new river | Everyone left London for good | Nothing, it was only a whiff
test: ★★★ Jess: «Snow just guessed the pump lol.» What did he really do? | Mapped the cases and saw them gather round one pump | Smelled every pump in London till one smelled bad | Asked the queen which of the pumps to close | Closed every single pump in the whole of England
test: ★★★ Grandpa Earl: «Back in my day... well, my grandad's day, every town kept its own time, and the trains changed nothing.» True? | No, railways put towns on London time | Yes, every town keeps its own time still | Yes, the change came with the internet | No, a law of Napoleon's made them change
test: ★★★ «Tea, silver, opium, war, Hong Kong.» Which link explains why British traders brought opium to China? | They wanted silver back after paying for tea | They wanted to grow opium in the fields of southern China | China's emperor asked Britain for opium | Opium was the price agreed for Hong Kong
test: ★★★ Priya in Mumbai: «Sputnik went up, so America just chilled, lah.» What did the USA really do? | Set up NASA and raced to the Moon | Bought Sputnik from the Soviets | Gave up on space travel for good | Built a copy of Sputnik and stopped
test: ★★★ Lucy: «Women got the vote in 1918 only coz of the war, end of.» Better answer? | The war helped, but campaigners had fought for decades | Right; nobody asked for votes before 1914 | No; the war stopped women from ever voting | No; British women could always vote
test: ★★★ Tom: «Writing came first, then people invented farming so they'd have something to write about.» What's wrong? | Farming came thousands of years before writing | Nothing; writing is much older than farming | Writing and farming began on the same day | Farming came only after the printing press
test: ★★★ Amara: «The «Kobzar» came out in 1840, right after the Opium War began in 1839, so the war caused it.» Fair? | No; after is not because | Yes; every event causes the next one | Yes; the «Kobzar» is about the Opium War | No; the «Kobzar» caused the Opium War
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which writing was first developed in Sumer. | Records of goods and taxes | The composition of poetry | The sending of letters abroad | The recording of games
exam: Select the factor which, following the Black Death, enabled surviving labourers to demand higher wages. | A shortage of workers | A great surplus of gold | New royal laws on pay | Unusually good harvests
exam: Determine the immediate occasion, as distinct from the deeper causes, of the First World War. | The shooting of Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo | The rivalry of two great alliances | The landing at Gallipoli in 1915 | The Christmas truce of 1914
exam: Ascertain the source of the cholera outbreak identified by John Snow in 1854. | One water pump | Bad smells in the air | Rats on the ships | Cold winter weather
